    "chunk_size_bytes": 1901,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Speaker, it is no exaggeration to say that this economic stability of our country was built during a most difficult period. The war raging in the Middle East is becoming more prolonged than ever before, creating great uncertainty in the world. The closure of the Strait of Hormuz has affected nearly 20 per cent of global petroleum shipments. Accordingly, the increase in fuel prices, premiums and shipping and insurance charges had a very adverse impact on our energy market. We have still not been able to free ourselves from the pressure this created on our economy and on people's lives. As a result, more than Rs. 147 billion was spent to minimize the impact on the people and on economic stability.\n\nWe have been able to build economic stability while facing all these adverse circumstances.\n\nHon. Speaker, as forecast by the World Meteorological Organization, it has been confirmed that there is a 100 per cent probability that the El Ni\u00f1o weather condition that has now developed will reach its peak in February, 2027. This El Ni\u00f1o weather condition\n\n349 350\n\n\u0db4\u0ddc\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nposes a risk of adverse impacts on agriculture, food prices, water resources and hydropower. We consider it one of our foremost responsibilities to identify such risks that may arise both globally and domestically in advance, and to further stabilize the economy so that it is capable of facing them.\n\nOur target is not only to manage today's economic challenges, but also to transform the country into one where future generations will never again have to face an economic crisis such as the one that prevailed before. The essence of our policy statement, \"A Thriving Nation \u2013 A Beautiful Life\", is not merely to improve the performance of economic indicators, but to create a society in which people can live with dignity and pride, and a country that has confidence in its future."
