    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "4187de1f3ecc69d37d90b7d56d135125",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1906,
    "chunk_text": "Hon. Speaker, the above facts are not the only criteria that measure the success of our fiscal policy. The Government's support for and care of distressed children, the elderly, persons with disabilities, those suffering from chronic illnesses, low-income households and families who have lost their livelihoods due to disasters are equally important.\n\nEconomic transformation does not mean presenting yet another list of expenditures. It means that every investment made by the Government, every tax concession granted, every borrowing undertaken and every social protection programme proposed will be assessed on the basis of the economic and social benefits generated through them.\n\nIt is not our objective to make all the sacrifices that the people bore during the economic crisis, and continue to bear even at this moment, into permanent sacrifices. It is our duty to build economic stability, better jobs, fair incomes, a quality Public Service, a safe social environment and a sustainable future for future generations.\n\n2. Seven Strategic Objectives of the 2027\n\nBudget\n\nHon. Speaker, when the Budget for 2026 was presented to this House, we emphasized the need to build an economy that can move forward unshaken in the face of internal and external challenges. At the end of 2025, Cyclone Ditwah severely affected the lives of our people, production activities and infrastructure. Due to the Middle East conflict that escalated in the first half of 2026, new pressure exerted on energy prices, import costs and supply chains.\n\nIn facing a disaster such as Cyclone Ditwah, what was important for the Government was that we had built fiscal space in advance. Through this, we were able to strengthen the financial capacity required to provide relief to the people affected by the disaster, to maintain essential services and to deploy resources for reconstruction activities according to priorities."
