    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/businessdocs/english/24650_english_2026-11-12.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-12-2026-11-12-0404",
    "chunk_index": 404,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "c72df208f1049aacf0505a4efced9fb9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1804,
    "chunk_text": "In facing a disaster such as Cyclone Ditwah, what was important for the Government was that we had built fiscal space in advance. Through this, we were able to strengthen the financial capacity required to provide relief to the people affected by the disaster, to maintain essential services and to deploy resources for reconstruction activities according to priorities.\n\nThis need becomes even clearer when we look at the current state of the world economy. According to the World Economic Outlook issued by the International Monetary Fund in October, 2026, global economic growth is expected to slow down to 3.1 per cent in 2026. Risks such as the escalation of conflicts, rising energy and food prices and uncertainty in international trade still persist.\n\nWe need a long-term plan capable of withstanding these conditions.\n\n2.1 Safeguarding Macroeconomic Stability and\n\nStrengthening the Resilience of the Economy\n\nWhile safeguarding macroeconomic and fiscal stability, our main objective is to ensure debt sustainability and to prepare Sri Lanka to face future internal and external shocks. For this, sustainable Government revenue, prudent expenditure management, planned debt repayments, adequate fiscal space and strong foreign reserves are essential. It is also important to maintain adequate coordination between fiscal and monetary policies, while respecting the legal mandate of the Central Bank of Sri Lanka in maintaining price stability.\n\nThe Central Government debt stood at 114 per cent in 2022, the year in which the economy was plunged into crisis. What is taken into consideration in debt sustainability is the capacity to repay debt relative to GDP and Government revenue, and whether there is adequate liquidity to settle the debt servicing obligations falling due in the near term."
