    "md5": "04ccea88d66a6dd835e4ee95d3316cf3",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2034,
    "chunk_text": "It is essential to build an efficient Government machinery that provides a quality service to the people, facilitates investment and production activities, and is accountable to the law and transparent. Modernizing public financial management, State-owned Enterprise and asset management, tax administration and the procurement process are essential elements of this. Minimizing corruption and unnecessary administrative delays, while strengthening the rule of law and accountability, is equally important to enhance public trust and to safeguard public resources.\n\n3. Medium-Term Macroeconomic and Fiscal\n\nPath\n\n355 356\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0d85\u0db1\u0dd4\u0dbb \u0d9a\u0dd4\u0db8\u0ddc\u0dbb \u0daf\u0dd2\u0dc3\u0ddc \u0ddc\u0dba\u0d9a \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0ddc ]\n\n2026 \u0db1 \u0ddc\u0dc0\u0dd0\u0db8\u0dca\u0db6\u0dbb\u0dca 12\n\nOur objective is to maintain Government revenue at a level not less than 16 per cent of GDP in the medium term, and to gradually increase it to about 20 per cent in the long term. To achieve these targets, the necessary reforms will be implemented to broaden the tax base, rationalize tax exemptions and incentives, digitalize tax administration and strengthen tax compliance.\n\nIn accordance with the Public Financial Management Act, we will take steps to maintain medium-term expenditure in line with the primary expenditure ceiling. However, due to the additional allocations required to restore the infrastructure and people's livelihood damaged by Cyclone Ditwah, primary expenditure in 2027 is expected to increase temporarily to 13.6 per cent of GDP. While managing these special expenditure requirements within the statutory fiscal framework, we also expect to bring primary expenditure back to 13 per cent of GDP from 2028 onwards.\n\nHon. Speaker, we expect to maintain public investment at a level exceeding 4 per cent of GDP from 2027 onwards. In 2027, a Capital Expenditure of Rs. 2,000 billion has been allocated, amounting to total public investment of 5.12 per cent of GDP, comprising 4.48 per cent through the Budget and 0.64 per cent through the Supplementary Estimate for Ditwah."
