    "chunk_text": "transfer to the Central Government the District General Hospitals of Mannar, Mullaitivu, Vavuniya, Kilinochchi, Gampaha and Avissawella, which are administered by the provincial councils, out of the 20 District General Hospitals.\n\n\u2022 Provisions of Rs. 65,587 million are required to fully meet, within three years, the requirement for high-end biomedical equipment identified through a formal study, including MRI, CT and Cath Lab, which are necessary to enhance diagnostic and treatment services in all hospitals. For this, it is proposed to allocate Rs. 5,000 million in addition to the provisions of Rs. 15,500 million allocated for the year 2027.\n\n\u2022 There are 400 ambulances in the health service that are more than 20 years old, of which around 50 per cent are more than 25 years old. In order to ensure a better quality and more efficient service to the people, it is proposed to allocate Rs. 3,000 million in 2027 to add new ambulances to the system in place of the old ambulances. In addition, 25 ambulances through Asian Development Bank loan assistance and 20 new ambulances as a grant from Japan, coordinated by the Asian Development Bank, will be added within the next year.\n\n\u2022 100 new ambulances will be added in 2027 under Indian assistance of Rs. 1,725 million to expand the 1990 Suwa Seriya service, which consists of 319 ambulances.\n\n\u2022 An organ transplant centre, including for the liver, at a cost of Rs. 3,155 million, will be established within the National Hospital of Colombo with the financial contribution of local and foreign institutions and organizations. Rs. 50 million has been allocated in 2027 for the initial activities.\n\n\u2022 Rs. 400 million has been allocated to implement widely within the country the biological Wolbachia dengue vector control method with the technical contribution of the World Mosquito Programme - WMP - and the co-financing contribution of the Government, to control the dengue epidemic."
