    "chunk_text": "\u2022 It is proposed to establish a centre within the Faculty of Medicine, Ragama to train doctors in Robotic Surgery. It is expected to provide training for the work of this centre in collaboration with foreign universities. It is proposed to allocate Rs. 250 million for this purpose.\n\n23. Tourism Sector\n\nThe diversity of Sri Lanka's tourist attractions is the most important and strongest potential of the country's tourism industry. Tourism is one of the main sectors that activates the country's economy, earns foreign exchange and generates employment. The earnings from tourist arrivals by September this year amounted to US Dollars 2,245 million. Therefore, attention has been paid to uplifting the rural economy and generating employment through the tourism industry.\n\n\u2022 Rs. 300 million will be deployed from the funds of the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority to review the progress of the Southern Mist Triangle project, which is implemented centred on the Sinharaja evergreen rainforest covering the Galle, Matara and Ratnapura Districts in the Southern and Sabaragamuwa Provinces, and to further strengthen it.\n\n\u2022 Rs. 35 million has been allocated, in addition to the capital contribution of Rs.\n\n389 390\n\n\u0db4\u0ddc\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\n\u2022 133 million provided by the Sri Lanka Tourism Development Authority - SLTDA - , to establish a centralized online e-ticketing system covering the main tourist attractions of Sri Lanka.\n\n\u2022 With the financial assistance of the World Bank, at a cost of US Dollars 77 million, the historic and ancient buildings located around Colombo Fort will be restored in a manner that protects their historical value and developed as a tourism zone.\n\n\u2022 It is proposed to allocate Rs. 500 million to develop the Indian-assisted trilingual school premises in Polonnaruwa into a hotel school, including trilingual teaching, and to develop the Pinnawala Hotel School."
  },
