    "md5": "1e7e46a7c2e6f4ea23c142a04839f288",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1922,
    "chunk_text": "\u2022 The need to efficiently maintain drainage systems, carry out emergency operations during floods and systematically maintain small canals and drainage systems in order to minimize flood risk in urban low-lying areas is increasing. For this, steps will be taken to obtain multipurpose machinery worth Rs. 1,250 million for the Sri Lanka Land Development Corporation, and it is proposed to provide Rs. 625 million of this as Government equity capital.\n\nUrban Flood Control\n\n\u2022 Urban flooding is caused by the failure to properly maintain the main canals in the City of Colombo and its suburbs in a timely manner, as well as the insufficient capacity of the canals that carry rainwater. In addition to the flood mitigation projects already underway in Kolonnawa and Oliyumulla areasassociated with the City of Colombo, several other key initiatives are slated to commence in 2027. These include the Mudun Ela Project - aimed at flood prevention in the Wattala, Kelaniya, and Biyagama areas -, a project to upgrade the stormwater drainage system in Piliyandala and Kesbewa, as well as the rehabilitation projects for the St. Sebastian Canal and the Kirulapona Canal, both designed to control flooding in the North Colombo region.\n\n\u2022 Additionally, there are plans to develop a section of the Hamilton Canal. Furthermore, projects to improve the Bindunu Ela - canal - system in Wadduwa and Molligoda - Kalutara District - as well as rainwater drainage systems in the Gampaha and Ratnapura Districts, and in Dikwella, Beruwala and Rathgama are set to commence in 2027.\n\n\u2022 Rs. 3,200 million has been allocated for projects aimed at improving urban flood control and environmental systems, including the restoration of identified canals in Colombo and its suburban\n\n397 398\n\n\u0db4\u0ddc\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\ncities and the improvement of the stormwater drainage system adjacent to the Piliyandala\u2013Kesbewa by-road."
