    "chunk_text": "For this, steps will be taken to digitalize and conserve sacred places, historical artefacts and cultural identities through digital technological innovations such as 3D laser scanning used internationally as well as locally. This will make it possible to permanently maintain accurate three-dimensional models of the national heritage even in the face of natural disasters or weather impacts.\n\nRs. 7,000 million has been allocated in this Budget for the promotion and development of artistic activities associated with various ethnicities and religions.\n\n\u2022 Funds have been allocated to the Ministry of Housing and Water Supply to implement the \u201cYathiwara\u201d housing programme for parents, which was initiated in 2026, during the year 2027.\n\n\u2022 The Government has provided provisions of Rs. 722 million for the rehabilitation of 1,328 places of religious worship damaged by the Ditwah disaster, comprising 758 Buddhist temples, 272 Hindu places of worship, 199 Muslim places of worship and 99 Christian places of worship. In addition, among the 1,328 destroyed places of religious worship, Rs. 40 million from the Buddha Sasana Fund for 30 Buddhist temples, and Rs. 523 million from the Central Cultural Fund for archaeological sites, including places of religious worship, are being utilized for rehabilitation, and a temple is also being rehabilitated through the Anagarika Dharmapala Trust with funds donated by the Maha Bodhi Society of India.\n\n401 402\n\n\u0db4\u0ddc\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dd2\u0db1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\n32. Mass Media\n\n\u2022 Provisions have been allocated amounting to Rs. 400 million to the Sri Lanka Rupavahini Corporation and Rs. 489 million to the Independent TelevisionNetwork, for implementing digitalization activities, improving broadcasting facilities and purchasing modern equipment and technological tools. Rs. 384 million has been allocated to the Sri Lanka Broadcasting Corporation for Recurrent Expenditure."
